 "diamond": {
  "adversary": "GRU Unit 26165 (85th GTsSS) [S1][S2].",
  "capability": "GooseEgg; public exploits (CVE-2023-23397, CVE-2022-38028); password spraying; spear-phishing; Exchange mailbox permission abuse; X-Agent, Zebrocy, LoJax and related families [S1][S2][S3].",
  "infrastructure": "Actor-controlled and compromised infrastructure for phishing and C2; compromised IP cameras used as collection sensors [S2].",
  "victim": "Ukrainian and Western government, defense, logistics, transportation, IT and education organizations [S1][S2].",
  "socioPolitical": "Military intelligence support to Russia's war in Ukraine, especially visibility into Western aid flows [S2].",
  "technology": "Weak authentication on mail systems and widespread, poorly secured IoT cameras [S2]."
 },
 "gaps": [
  "Primary text of the 21 May 2025 joint advisory (AA25-141A) could not be fetched (CISA and BSI blocked); verify CVE list, camera counts and named countries against the PDF.",
  "Earlier landmark events (2016 DNC intrusion and the 2018 DOJ indictment of GRU officers, Drovorub advisory, Ubiquiti EdgeRouter disruption in 2024) are not yet sourced in this profile.",
  "Whether camera-derived intelligence has been used to cue physical sabotage or strikes is an agency assessment, not a confirmed event.",
  "Some remaining aliases (e.g. Grizzly Steppe, which named joint APT28/APT29 activity) are ambiguous.",
  "Primary-source check (2026-10-18): AA25-141A confirms GRU 85th GTsSS unit 26165, password spraying, spearphishing, Exchange mailbox-permission changes, and camera targeting at Ukrainian border crossings. Other S4 claims (e.g. BfV coverage) were not checked against it."
 ],
 "implications": {
  "jipoe": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. STEP 1: The cyberspace OE includes commercial logistics providers, rail and port operators, and the IoT cameras along supply routes into Ukraine and NATO's eastern flank. These are intelligence collection points for the adversary. STEP 2: Compromised cameras turn the physical terrain itself into adversary ISR, which affects OPSEC for movements. STEP 3: The template is password spraying or phishing against mail accounts, then mailbox permission changes for persistence, then collection on shipments and schedules. In parallel, mass compromise of exposed cameras. STEP 4: MLCOA is continued collection on aid logistics to inform Russian targeting and diplomacy. MDCOA is using collected movement data to cue sabotage or strikes on aid shipments or nodes. Candidate indicators: new mailbox delegation or folder permissions, spray activity against logistics firms, and anomalous access to camera management interfaces near routes [S2].",
  "jcc2": "Analyst assessment derived from cited sources; validate before use. (1) Treat commercial partners' camera and mail exposure as part of force-protection planning for movements. (2) JCC2 situational awareness should take in partner and industry reporting on logistics-sector intrusions, since the victims are mostly private. (3) Authorities: hardening of commercial and allied camera networks is a partner-nation and owner responsibility. DoD influence is through contracts, requirements and liaison. (4) OPSEC: plan movement security assuming adversary visibility through compromised cameras on routes."
 },
